          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01YQKikDZgsfYV7E4J7TizHm",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, particularly hybrid retrieval combining lexical and semantic search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One authoritative source on hybrid retrieval; reciprocal rank fusion component verified but not independently corroborated.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines two complementary search strategies to overcome the limitations of either method alone:\\n\\n- **Sparse lexical search** (e.g., BM25): excels at exact term matching, particularly for rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs, technical jargon).\\n- **Dense vector search**: captures semantic similarity and paraphrases, understanding intent behind natural language.\\n\\nThe two ranked result sets are then merged, commonly using **reciprocal rank fusion** (RRF), which balances contributions from both rankers without requiring tuning of per-method weights.\\n\\n### Performance Characteristics\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix rare identifiers with natural language intent. This makes it the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, where queries often contain both structured references (SKUs, error codes) and conversational semantic content.\\n\\n### Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nUse reciprocal rank fusion for robust retrieval. This merging strategy treats sparse and dense results symmetrically, avoiding parameter tuning while maintaining stability across diverse query types.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Source: eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-02 \\u2014 description of hybrid retrieval combining BM25 and dense search with RRF merging in production RAG systems.\\n\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
